612
00:43:07,310 --> 00:43:10,884
So Mac OS builds taking six doesn't matter because they're happening in parallel anyways.

613
00:43:11,182 --> 00:43:11,940
But now it does.

614
00:43:12,302 --> 00:43:19,620
So I did yet another thread where I cleaned up the way that we were handling signing to cut the build times by meaningful amounts.

615
00:43:20,046 --> 00:43:21,568
It's not the 81% it says here.

616
00:43:21,648 --> 00:43:25,860
This is the number of signing calls, but the overall build time dropped by like a minute and a half, if I recall.

617
00:43:26,128 --> 00:43:31,556
I know these things probably seem a bit silly, but I'm trying to emphasize this pattern to you guys.

618
00:43:31,822 --> 00:43:51,088
If you find where there is friction in your flows, if you pay close attention to what keeps you from merging things, what keeps you from being excited to hit that button, what risks are blocking you, what mental burdens and barriers are keeping you from checking the code, what friction is making it worse to do.

619
00:43:51,168 --> 00:43:53,476
So you delay it more and more until it never happens.

620
00:43:53,806 --> 00:43:59,008
All of these are opportunities to improve your project, improve your workflow, improve the tools you use to build.

621
00:43:59,214 --> 00:44:07,408
And as you make more of these improvements, you'll feel that exponential growth and how much you can ship and how much confidence you have in what you're shipping.

622
00:44:07,568 --> 00:44:18,500
To go back to my contribution rate, I was able to more than double my throughput while on vacation because more and more of these changes landed and it got easier and easier for me to verify my own work.

623
00:44:18,798 --> 00:44:20,196
But I need to be real with you guys.

624
00:44:20,622 --> 00:44:24,036
Mistakes do absolutely still happen.

625
00:44:24,398 --> 00:44:29,380
Sometimes things merge that shouldn't, especially if we're not putting a lot of effort into reading every line of code anymore.

626
00:44:29,806 --> 00:44:33,092
Most of the bugs could not have reasonably been caught by just reading code.

627
00:44:33,328 --> 00:44:42,372
As such, it's really important to have some form of a safety net, a way to catch the mistakes so they don't affect all your users or cause real problems.

628
00:44:42,766 --> 00:44:50,660
We have an admittedly not perfect solution for this with T3 code, but due to the unique nature of our audience, it's proven to be pretty effective.

629
00:44:51,054 --> 00:44:53,248
That solution is right here.

630
00:44:54,062 --> 00:44:54,884
The nightly builds.

631
00:44:55,342 --> 00:44:57,380
Don't let the term mislead you.

632
00:44:57,902 --> 00:45:00,580
Nightly builds happen every three hours for us.

633
00:45:01,454 --> 00:45:07,140
So when a change lands, that change will be out in the nightly within three hours, often less.

634
00:45:07,502 --> 00:45:09,536
We have a dedicated set of nightly users.

635
00:45:09,616 --> 00:45:13,668
It's a few thousand of our surprisingly big 200,000 plus user base.

636
00:45:14,158 --> 00:45:20,100
So if we do have anything go wrong in the nightly, we immediately get a ton of reports about it.

637
00:45:20,496 --> 00:45:23,536
And let's be frank, nightly users know what they're getting into.

638
00:45:23,726 --> 00:45:28,228
Things will sometimes break, things will sometimes get removed after they're in.

639
00:45:28,590 --> 00:45:34,016
The nightly builds give users the feeling that they're staying more on top of what we're doing, which is awesome and true.

640
00:45:34,350 --> 00:45:39,748
I saw a lot of people who were disappointed that I had the big announcement for the new release because they already had all the features they were on nightly.

641
00:45:40,096 --> 00:45:46,884
The nightly builds are awesome because they give us a shitload of useful information about what may have broken.

642
00:45:47,182 --> 00:45:55,588
As such, our stable releases pretty much never have real regressions because they get noticed so heavily during this nightly testing window.

643
00:45:55,918 --> 00:45:57,056
There are other ways to do this.

644
00:45:57,166 --> 00:46:05,056
You can do slow rollouts where you only roll out changes to 1% of your users, and if you start seeing errors or issues, you immediately roll back and go figure them out.

645
00:46:05,296 --> 00:46:10,256
You can have a formal QA team that does thorough testing of everything before the code goes out.

646
00:46:10,670 --> 00:46:13,860
The key things I want you to take away from this point are, I guess, twofold.

647
00:46:14,222 --> 00:46:22,500
The first is that you need a way for the changes that break to not have to affect a ton of users and be easy to catch and fix.

648
00:46:22,830 --> 00:46:28,452
But the more important piece here that is a little hidden underneath, I want you to de-risk the merge button.

649
00:46:28,816 --> 00:46:35,780
You shouldn't even be looking at the pull request until a bunch of agents have relatively high confidence that it's ready for you to look at.

650
00:46:36,206 --> 00:46:40,452
I want you to have better ways to test the changes yourself so that you have confidence too.

651
00:46:40,750 --> 00:46:45,828
I don't want you to have to worry when you hit merge that if something breaks, it's going to affect all of your users.

652
00:46:46,350 --> 00:46:53,924
So ideally, you will reduce as much friction as possible before you even get in the PR so that you have relatively high confidence going in, that things will work.

653
00:46:54,096 --> 00:47:02,692
And then after you've built a bit more confidence, that you're hitting with no fear, because if it does break, the break will be caught and addressed fast enough.

654
00:47:03,086 --> 00:47:07,204
In order to de-risk merge, you need to address both sides.

655
00:47:07,598 --> 00:47:10,976
You need to address what makes going to the PR in the first place feel useless.

656
00:47:11,278 --> 00:47:15,936
You need to address the risks when the PR seems ready to go that still make merge scary.

657
00:47:16,206 --> 00:47:17,764
And our boy Agor has caught on.

658
00:47:18,446 --> 00:47:22,596
This has been one of my philosophies since day zero, since way before I was doing content.

659
00:47:23,182 --> 00:47:25,092
Safety nets, not guardrails.

660
00:47:25,582 --> 00:47:26,852
I'm not saying don't test.

661
00:47:27,056 --> 00:47:32,068
We have a shitload of tests in T3 code, and we're probably merging more tests than we are features nowadays.

662
00:47:32,430 --> 00:47:35,296
But the safety nets are what make T3 code so safe.

663
00:47:35,630 --> 00:47:44,036
The fact that a change that breaks things will be called out and noticed before it hits even a thousandth of our users makes it so much easier to hit merge and worry later.

664
00:47:44,336 --> 00:48:04,256
I think all of these pieces are what has allowed me to be as productive with T3 code, as I have been, from the inbox style sidebar that I treat like a to-do list, to all the systems I have built for verifying changes, to all the unique ways I burn tokens in order to be more confident in my work, to the ways I can ship with confidence and the ability to roll back.

665
00:48:04,256 --> 00:48:05,296
if things do go wrong.

666
00:48:05,582 --> 00:48:12,416
All of this has enabled a part-time dev like me, as well as the full-time devs on my team, to ship way faster and way more confidently.

667
00:48:12,654 --> 00:48:18,148
And I don't really care if you're using T3 code or not, because the people who are have already seen the difference and are very happy with it.

668
00:48:18,510 --> 00:48:36,016
I know that a lot of this might have come off as an unintentional plug for T3 code, but what I really wanted to be was a showcase of how I improve the tools I use by running into problems and also the workflows I've built around T3 Code in order to be as productive and effective shipping software as possible.

669
00:48:36,334 --> 00:48:41,616
And I'll be frank, a lot of why I made this is I want to look back to it in six months and see how silly some of the things I was doing are.

670
00:48:41,806 --> 00:48:50,736
Because every time I make one of these workflow videos, I get told by a lot of people how helpful they are, even a year later, when they've long since stopped being helpful.

671
00:48:51,214 --> 00:48:53,316
Man, I need to do one of these every three months now, don't I?

672
00:48:53,536 --> 00:48:54,096
Huh.

673
00:48:55,182 --> 00:48:56,256
What a fun journey.

674
00:48:56,494 --> 00:49:02,468
Hopefully you have a better idea of how I've managed to stay productive with the utter chaos that is my life and my coding setups.

675
00:49:03,150 --> 00:49:04,176
Maybe you learned some things here.

676
00:49:04,336 --> 00:49:05,936
Maybe you hate everything I just showed you.

677
00:49:06,126 --> 00:49:07,044
Let me know in the comments.

678
00:49:07,246 --> 00:49:12,736
And if you have pro tips that are things that could benefit my workflows that I'm not trying yet, please let me know those as well.

679
00:49:12,840 --> 00:49:18,308
because I'm always looking for opportunities to improve, streamline, and be more effective in this new era of development.

680
00:49:18,702 --> 00:49:19,536
Let me know how y'all feel.

681
00:49:19,776 --> 00:49:21,560
until next time, peace nerds.

